star wars fans argue so much about the  successes of the franchise it's easy to  let the true failures slip your mind the  connect game the 3d re-releases detours  those and more ahead  if you thought star wars didn't enter  the world of animated television until  the 21st century you'd be wrong but you  wouldn't be alone either after all  lucasfilm has largely buried the  company's first foray into this  particular genre one that occurred as  far back as the 1980s  that's when star wars droids attempted  to make waves in the world of saturday  morning programming however the show  struggled for a number of reasons for  one it had a pretty hefty price tag with  each episode running between five  hundred thousand and six hundred  thousand dollars and while c-3po and  r2d2 are always a hit with the fans none  of the side characters were really  clicking artist brian lemay later told  sci-fi wire  the secondary characters that the droids  were involved with were not really  strong characters they were just these  characters that were thrown in to carry  them through an adventure of sorts  worse still this intensely  family-friendly show lacked any sort of  edge future star wars cartoons wouldn't  be afraid to spice things up a little  but droids was allowed no such  flourishes the man behind c-3po anthony  daniels even once complained  you can't put a custard pie in my face  because of what they call imitative  behavior they don't want children saying  oh we saw it on the cartoon we thought  it was okay  perhaps unsurprisingly droids didn't  last more than a single season  while the clone wars was on the air  plans got underway for the show to spin  off into another animated show a series  of clone wars episodes focusing on a  collection of young padawans aired  during the series fifth season in early  2012. this arc was supposed to establish  a new group of characters who would  later star in their own show the  episodes that introduced these figures  centered on the younglings trying to  build their own lightsabers with the  help of a socatano and a wise droid  voiced by david tennant so let us begin  there is much more work to be done  there was enough confidence in this  premise that the episodes were even  planned to air as a single tv movie  before leading into a tv show mirroring  the way in which clone wars itself was  introduced to the world however for  reasons unknown this storyline was  relegated to a string of traditional  episodes while the spin-off was put on  the back burner this was in spite of the  new program being something of a passion  project for george lucas himself the  production fell further into the rear  view once disney bought lucasfilm and  set up its own slate of kid-oriented  star wars cartoons  with the creation of xbox's connect a  new world of video game possibilities  suddenly opened up gaming that relied on  your body movements rather than a  controller was a prospect that seemed so  exciting at the time and it wasn't long  before thoughts turned to how the star  wars universe could utilize this idea  after all star wars and video games have  been a lucrative pairing for decades now  so why not embrace this new technology  unfortunately rather than using the  kinect controllers to make a new  action-adventure title lucasarts  produced connect star wars a grab bag of  different play modes ranging from  padawan training to  dance-offs  inevitably connect star wars failed to  connect with the general public and  critics were less than impressed some  praise was offered for certain aspects  of the gameplay but the project just  didn't come together like it was  supposed to as it stands the only part  of connect star wars to have had any  lasting impact is one of its dance off  tunes titled i'm han solo this parody of  ryden solo became infamous with the av  club calling it quote one of the worst  pieces of star wars licensing ever not  great as far as legacies go  for a moment it looked like the 